			</div><div class="et_pb_module et_pb_post_content et_pb_post_content_0_tb_body blog-post-content">
				
				
				
				
				<p>Furnace not heating on the first cold night of the year? Usually it’s one of six things: a thermostat setting, a clogged filter, a power problem, the gas supply, a dirty flame sensor, or a bad igniter. You can rule out the first few in about 10 minutes. No tools needed.</p>
<p>If those all check out and the house is still cold, the problem’s inside the furnace. That’s our cue.</p>
<p><strong>No heat tonight?</strong> Air Zone Experts handles emergency furnace repair across Little Elm, Frisco, and North DFW. Call <a href="tel:2144309059"><strong>(214) 430-9059</strong></a>.</p>
<h2><strong>When Should You Stop and Get Out?</strong></h2>
<p>Some symptoms aren’t a troubleshooting job. Don’t touch the furnace if you notice either of these:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rotten-egg smell.</strong> That’s the odorant added to natural gas. Leave the house, skip the light switches, and call 911 or your gas utility’s emergency line once you’re outside.</li>
<li><strong>Carbon monoxide alarm going off.</strong> Everyone out, pets too. Then call 911.</li>
</ul>
<p>A burning or electrical smell is different but still serious. Shut the furnace off at its service switch and call us. Same goes for a breaker that trips again the second you reset it.</p>
<p>None of that going on? Good. Work through the checks below in order.</p>
<h2><strong>Start With the Thermostat</strong></h2>
<p>After a long Texas summer, your furnace hasn’t run in months. So the first cold front is when thermostat mix-ups show up. Lots of them.</p>
<p>Check the mode first. It should say <strong>Heat</strong>. Plenty of homes are still sitting on Cool from August (we see it every year).</p>
<p>Then bump the setpoint a few degrees above the room temperature. The furnace needs a reason to fire.</p>
<p>Now look at the fan setting:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>On</strong> keeps the blower running nonstop, even between heating cycles. So the air coming out feels cold half the time.</li>
<li><strong>Auto</strong> only runs the blower while the furnace is actually heating. That’s what you want.</li>
</ul>
<p>Blank or dim screen? Swap the batteries. On a smart thermostat, look for a schedule or eco mode overriding you. A dropped Wi-Fi connection can do it too.</p>
<h2><strong>Is the Air Filter Clogged?</strong></h2>
<p>Probably the most common reason a furnace runs but won’t heat.</p>
<p>Here’s what happens. A packed filter chokes the airflow, and heat builds up inside the furnace. A safety part called the <strong>high-limit switch</strong> notices and shuts the burners down so nothing overheats. The blower doesn’t get the memo. It keeps pushing air through the vents, just not warm air.</p>
<p>Pull the filter out and hold it up to a light. Can’t see through it? Replace it. During heating season, check it once a month, and match the size printed on the old frame.</p>
<h2><strong>Does the Furnace Have Power?</strong></h2>
<p>Start at the breaker panel. If the furnace breaker tripped, reset it once.</p>
<p>Next, find the service switch. It looks like a regular light switch on or near the furnace. In a lot of DFW homes, the furnace lives in the attic, so that switch is up there too (easy to bump when you’re hauling holiday boxes around).</p>
<p>One more spot people miss: the front panel on the furnace. It presses a safety switch. If the panel’s loose or sitting crooked, the furnace won’t start. Period.</p>
<h2><strong>Is Gas Getting to the Furnace?</strong></h2>
<p>Find the valve on the gas line going into the unit. The handle should run <strong>parallel</strong> to the pipe. Sideways means closed.</p>
<p>Next, try your stove or water heater. If those won’t light either, the issue is gas coming into the house, not the furnace. Call your gas utility.</p>
<p>Got an older furnace with a standing pilot light? Most units today use electronic ignition, so that’s less common. If yours does have one and it’s out, follow the relighting steps printed on the furnace label. Won’t stay lit? Call us.</p>
<h2><strong>What Do You See and Hear When It Starts?</strong></h2>
<p>A gas furnace starts up in a set order. The inducer motor hums. The igniter glows or clicks. The burners light. Then, after a short pause, the blower kicks on.</p>
<p>Where that sequence stalls tells us a lot.</p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>What you notice</strong></td>
<td><strong>Likely cause</strong></td>
<td><strong>DIY or pro?</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Inducer runs, nothing ignites</td>
<td>Pressure switch, blocked vent, or a clogged condensate drain on high-efficiency units</td>
<td>Pro</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Igniter glows, no flame</td>
<td>Gas valve or gas supply</td>
<td>Check the valve, then call</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Burners light, then quit after a few seconds</td>
<td>Dirty flame sensor</td>
<td>Pro recommended</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>No glow, no click</td>
<td>Failed igniter or control board</td>
<td>Pro</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Burners run, blower never starts</td>
<td>Blower motor or capacitor</td>
<td>Pro</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Turns on and off over and over</td>
<td>Overheating from poor airflow, or a failing limit switch</td>
<td>Change the filter, then call</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Most furnaces also have a little status light you can see through a small window in the panel. Count the blinks. Then match them to the code chart stuck inside the panel door. Jot the code down before you call us. It saves real time on the diagnosis.</p>
<h2><strong>Should You Reset the Furnace?</strong></h2>
<p>Once. That’s it.</p>
<p>Flip the service switch off, wait about 30 seconds, and flip it back on. That clears a temporary lockout after a failed start.</p>
<p>Some furnaces have a red reset button, too. But if it’s the <strong>rollout switch</strong> that tripped, leave it alone. A tripped rollout switch means flames came out where they shouldn’t. That can point to a blocked flue or a cracked heat exchanger, and neither one gets fixed by pressing a button again.</p>
<p>Locks out a second time? Something’s failing.</p>
<h2><strong>When It’s Time to Call Us</strong></h2>
<p>Give us a call if you’ve done the checks above and there’s still no heat. Or if any of these sound familiar:</p>
<ul>
<li>The furnace locks out or short-cycles even after a reset.</li>
<li><strong>Blink code</strong> tied to ignition, the pressure switch, or the flame sensor.</li>
<li>Water pooling around a high-efficiency unit. In the attic, that gets expensive fast.</li>
<li>Grinding, squealing, or banging from the unit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not sure what you’re hearing? Try our <a href="/symptom-checker/">HVAC symptom checker</a> first. It’ll help you narrow things down.</p>
<p>We’re a family-owned HVAC company based right here in Little Elm. Our technicians are NATE-certified, and we work on just about every brand out there (Trane, Carrier, Lennox, Goodman, Rheem). You’ll get an upfront quote before we touch anything. And we only recommend what your system actually needs. Nothing extra.</p>
<p>Here’s more on our <a href="/heater-repair/">furnace repair in Little Elm and North DFW</a>.</p>
<h2><strong>Frequently Asked Questions</strong></h2>
<h3><strong>What is the most common cause of heat not working?</strong></h3>
<p>Honestly? The filter or the thermostat. Those two cover a big share of no-heat calls, and you can fix both yourself in minutes. Once you get inside the furnace, a dirty flame sensor is the usual suspect.</p>
<h3><strong>How do I reset a furnace heater?</strong></h3>
<p>Turn it off at the service switch or breaker, wait about 30 seconds, then turn it back on. Some furnaces also have a reset button on the blower motor or a rollout switch. Only reset once, though. If the furnace shuts down again, a part is failing, and pressing the button over and over just hides the problem.</p>
<h3><strong>What are the signs of a dying furnace?</strong></h3>
<p>Usually more than one. Watch for repairs that keep stacking up, rooms that heat unevenly, and short cycling. Rising gas bills are another clue. So are yellow burner flames instead of blue, plus new noises you didn’t hear last winter. Age plays into it as well, since many furnaces start needing more frequent repairs in their second decade.</p>
<h3><strong>How do you heat a house when the furnace is broken?</strong></h3>
<p>Close off rooms you’re not using and keep the curtains shut at night. Electric space heaters help, as long as they’re safety-listed with tip-over shutoff and sit at least 3 feet from anything that can burn. Never use a gas oven, grill, or generator for heat indoors. That’s a carbon monoxide risk, full stop.</p>
<h3><strong>What is the most expensive thing to fix on a furnace?</strong></h3>
<p>The heat exchanger. A cracked one can leak carbon monoxide, so this is often the point where replacing the furnace makes more sense than repairing it. Control boards and blower motors are other pricier fixes.</p>
<h2><strong>Get Your Heat Back</strong></h2>
<p>Most no-heat calls start with a simple check. Plenty end there too. If the thermostat, filter, power, and gas all look fine, the problem’s inside the furnace, and that’s where we come in. Call Air Zone Experts at <a href="tel:2144309059"><strong>(214) 430-9059</strong></a> for honest, upfront furnace repair in Little Elm, Frisco, and nearby North DFW communities.</p>

